Before analyzing an algorithm, it is important to identify the size of its input. Input size is commonly represented by n.

For example, if an algorithm receives an array containing 100 elements, then n is 100. If the array contains 1,000 elements, n is 1,000.

The purpose of Big-O analysis is to understand how the amount of work changes when n becomes larger. An algorithm that is efficient for a small input may become expensive when the input grows significantly.

For example, processing every element of an array requires work that grows with the number of elements:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
    process(numbers[i]);
}
```

If the array contains n elements, the loop performs approximately n iterations, giving it O(n) time complexity.

An algorithm has O(n), or linear time, complexity when its amount of work grows proportionally with the input size.

A simple example is searching for a value in an unsorted array:

```java
static int linearSearch(int[] numbers, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
        if (numbers[i] == target) {
            return i;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

In the worst case, the algorithm may inspect every element. If the array contains n elements, it can perform up to n comparisons.

Therefore, the worst-case time complexity is O(n). If the input size doubles, the amount of work can also roughly double.

An algorithm has O(log n) complexity when the problem size is repeatedly reduced by a constant factor. Binary search is a common example.

Binary search works on a sorted collection. Instead of examining every element, it checks the middle element and eliminates approximately half of the remaining search space after each comparison.

```java
static int binarySearch(int[] numbers, int target) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = numbers.length - 1;

    while (left <= right) {
        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (numbers[middle] == target) {
            return middle;
        }

        if (numbers[middle] < target) {
            left = middle + 1;
        } else {
            right = middle - 1;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

Because the search space is approximately halved on each iteration, the number of iterations grows logarithmically with n. Therefore, binary search has O(log n) time complexity.

An algorithm has O(n²), or quadratic time, complexity when its work grows approximately with the square of the input size. A common example is a nested loop where each loop processes approximately n elements.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < numbers.length; j++) {
        System.out.println(numbers[i] + " " + numbers[j]);
    }
}
```

If the array contains n elements, the outer loop runs n times and the inner loop also runs n times for each outer iteration.

The total number of operations is approximately n × n, which gives O(n²).

Quadratic algorithms can become expensive quickly as the input size increases.

An algorithm has O(n³) complexity when its work grows approximately with the cube of the input size. This can occur when three loops independently iterate over approximately n elements.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        for (int k = 0; k < n; k++) {
            process(i, j, k);
        }
    }
}
```

The operation inside the innermost loop can execute approximately n × n × n times, resulting in O(n³).

Cubic algorithms may be acceptable for very small input sizes but can become impractical as n grows.

O(n log n) is an important complexity class that appears in several efficient algorithms, especially sorting algorithms such as merge sort.

A typical O(n log n) algorithm combines linear processing with a logarithmic number of levels or operations.

For example, merge sort repeatedly divides an array into smaller portions and then merges those portions:

```java
static void mergeSort(int[] numbers, int left, int right) {
    if (left >= right) {
        return;
    }

    int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

    mergeSort(numbers, left, middle);
    mergeSort(numbers, middle + 1, right);

    merge(numbers, left, middle, right);
}
```

The recursive division creates approximately log n levels, while processing across each level is approximately O(n). This results in O(n log n) time complexity.

Exponential complexity occurs when the amount of work grows exponentially as the input size increases. O(2ⁿ) is a common example.

A simple recursive example is generating all subsets of a set:

```java
static void generateSubsets(int[] numbers, int index) {
    if (index == numbers.length) {
        return;
    }

    generateSubsets(numbers, index + 1);
    generateSubsets(numbers, index + 1);
}
```

The number of possible subsets of n elements is 2ⁿ. As a result, algorithms that explicitly explore all combinations can grow extremely quickly.

Exponential algorithms are often practical only for relatively small input sizes unless additional techniques reduce the amount of computation.

Big-O notation normally ignores constant multipliers because the focus is on how the algorithm grows as n becomes large.

For example, consider the following algorithm:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    process(i);
}

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    process(i);
}
```

The two loops perform approximately 2n operations. The formal expression can be written as O(2n), but the constant 2 is ignored, giving O(n).

Similarly, an expression such as 3n² + 10n + 20 is simplified to O(n²), because the n² term dominates the growth rate as n becomes large.

When an algorithm contains multiple terms with different growth rates, Big-O analysis focuses on the dominant term.

For example:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    process(i);
}

for (int i = 0; i < n * n; i++) {
    process(i);
}
```

The total work can be represented approximately as n + n². As n becomes larger, n² grows much faster than n.

Therefore:

O(n + n²) = O(n²)

The lower-order term is ignored because it has a smaller effect on the growth rate for large inputs.

Time complexity describes how the number of operations performed by an algorithm grows as the input size increases. It does not normally represent an exact measurement in seconds.

For example, consider an algorithm that scans an array once:

```java
static int sum(int[] numbers) {
    int total = 0;

    for (int number : numbers) {
        total += number;
    }

    return total;
}
```

The algorithm visits each element once. If the array contains n elements, approximately n iterations are performed, so the time complexity is O(n).

Time complexity helps determine whether an algorithm can handle the expected input constraints efficiently.

Space complexity describes how the additional memory required by an algorithm grows with the input size.

For example, an algorithm that creates a new array containing n elements requires additional memory proportional to n:

```java
static int[] copyArray(int[] numbers) {
    int[] copy = new int[numbers.length];

    for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
        copy[i] = numbers[i];
    }

    return copy;
}
```

The additional array contains n elements, so the auxiliary space complexity is O(n).

Space complexity is important because an algorithm can be fast while using a large amount of memory, or it can reduce memory usage at the cost of additional computation.

The amount of work performed by an algorithm can depend on the particular input. Therefore, complexity is sometimes analyzed using best-case, average-case, and worst-case scenarios.

Consider linear search:

```java
static int search(int[] numbers, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
        if (numbers[i] == target) {
            return i;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

If the target is the first element, the algorithm finishes immediately, giving a best-case time of O(1).

If the target is at the last position or does not exist, the algorithm may inspect every element, giving a worst-case time of O(n).

The average-case complexity depends on assumptions about how the input is distributed and where the target is expected to occur.

Big-O notation helps developers reason about how algorithms behave when input sizes become large. It is especially important when multiple solutions can produce the same result but have different efficiency characteristics.

For example, searching an unsorted array generally requires O(n) time, while binary search on a sorted array requires O(log n) time.

```java
// Linear search: O(n)
for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
    if (numbers[i] == target) {
        return i;
    }
}
```

```java
// Binary search: O(log n)
int left = 0;
int right = numbers.length - 1;

while (left <= right) {
    int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

    if (numbers[middle] == target) {
        return middle;
    }

    if (numbers[middle] < target) {
        left = middle + 1;
    } else {
        right = middle - 1;
    }
}
```

The appropriate solution depends on the problem constraints. Big-O analysis provides the information needed to understand these trade-offs and choose an algorithm that is suitable for the expected input size.

In imperative programming, instructions are normally executed sequentially unless a control-flow statement changes the execution path. The program starts at a particular point and executes one statement after another.

For example:

```java
int first = 10;
int second = 20;
int sum = first + second;

System.out.println(sum);
```

The program first creates first, then creates second, calculates their sum, and finally prints the result.

Algorithms often follow the same idea. A problem can be broken into a sequence of operations that must be performed in a particular order.

Variables allow an imperative program to store information that can be used and modified during execution. The current values stored in variables represent part of the program's state.

For example:

```java
int count = 0;

count = count + 1;
count = count + 1;

System.out.println(count);
```

The value of count changes from 0 to 1 and then to 2. This ability to modify state is a fundamental characteristic of imperative programming.

DSA algorithms frequently use variables to store indexes, counters, temporary values, accumulated results, and references to elements in data structures.

Assignment statements change the value associated with a variable. An algorithm can use assignments to update its state as it processes input.

For example, a simple algorithm can keep track of the largest value encountered so far:

```java
int max = numbers[0];

for (int i = 1; i < numbers.length; i++) {
    if (numbers[i] > max) {
        max = numbers[i];
    }
}
```

The variable max represents the current state of the algorithm. Whenever a larger value is found, max is updated.

This pattern appears frequently in DSA problems where an algorithm maintains information about elements processed so far.

Conditional statements allow an imperative program to make decisions. The most common constructs include if, else if, else, and switch.

For example:

```java
if (number > 0) {
    System.out.println("Positive");
} else if (number < 0) {
    System.out.println("Negative");
} else {
    System.out.println("Zero");
}
```

The program evaluates a condition and executes the appropriate block of instructions.

Conditional logic is essential in algorithms because programs often need to compare values, handle different cases, detect boundaries, and decide which operation should happen next.

Loops allow an imperative program to execute a set of instructions repeatedly. Common loop constructs include for, while, and do-while loops.

For example, an array can be traversed using a for loop:

```java
int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40};

for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(numbers[i]);
}
```

The loop processes each element of the array. This type of traversal is one of the most common patterns in DSA.

The number of loop iterations is also important when analyzing algorithm complexity. A loop that processes n elements generally contributes O(n) work, while nested loops may result in higher complexity.

Imperative programming allows loops to be placed inside other loops. This is called nesting and is frequently used when an algorithm needs to compare or process multiple combinations of elements.

For example:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
    for (int j = i + 1; j < numbers.length; j++) {
        System.out.println(numbers[i] + ", " + numbers[j]);
    }
}
```

The inner loop runs for different ranges depending on the value of i. Such nested loops are common in comparison-based algorithms.

When analyzing complexity, it is important to determine how many times the inner operation executes rather than automatically assuming every nested loop has exactly O(n²) complexity.

Functions or methods allow imperative programs to divide a larger problem into smaller reusable operations. A method can receive input through parameters, perform a sequence of instructions, and return a result.

For example:

```java
static int findMaximum(int[] numbers) {
    int max = numbers[0];

    for (int number : numbers) {
        if (number > max) {
            max = number;
        }
    }

    return max;
}
```

The findMaximum method encapsulates the steps required to find the largest value. Breaking algorithms into methods makes the implementation easier to read, test, reuse, and maintain.

Imperative programming works naturally with mutable data, where the contents of a data structure can change during execution. Many common DSA structures rely on this behavior.

For example, adding an element to a list changes the list's state:

```java
List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();

numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(20);
numbers.add(30);
```

The collection changes as new elements are added. Similarly, stack push and pop operations change the stack's contents, while linked-list insertion can change node references.

Understanding state changes is important when implementing and debugging mutable data structures.

In object-oriented imperative languages such as Java, variables can hold references to objects. Updating an object's fields changes the state represented by that object.

For example:

```java
class Node {
    int value;
    Node next;
}

Node first = new Node();
first.value = 10;

Node second = new Node();
second.value = 20;

first.next = second;
```

Here, first references one Node object and second references another. The assignment first.next = second connects the two nodes.

This type of state manipulation is fundamental to linked lists, trees, graphs, and other reference-based data structures.

Many DSA algorithms can be directly translated into imperative code. The algorithm specifies a sequence of operations, and the implementation uses variables, conditions, loops, and method calls to perform those operations.

For example, a linear search can be implemented as follows:

```java
static int linearSearch(int[] numbers, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
        if (numbers[i] == target) {
            return i;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

The algorithm maintains an index, examines each element, compares it with the target, and returns when a match is found.

This example demonstrates how an abstract algorithm becomes a sequence of concrete imperative instructions.

Imperative code makes it possible to see how many operations an algorithm performs. This is useful when analyzing time and space complexity.

Consider a simple traversal:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
    process(numbers[i]);
}
```

If numbers contains n elements, the loop processes each element once, giving the algorithm an O(n) time contribution.

If another loop is nested inside the first and both process approximately n elements, the amount of work may grow toward O(n²). Therefore, understanding imperative control flow is closely connected to understanding Big-O notation.

Imperative programming focuses on describing the steps needed to produce a result, while declarative programming focuses more on describing the desired result without specifying every execution step.

For example, an imperative approach can explicitly iterate through a collection:

```java
List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();

for (int number : numbers) {
    if (number > 10) {
        result.add(number);
    }
}
```

A more declarative approach can use a stream operation:

```java
List<Integer> result = numbers.stream()
        .filter(number -> number > 10)
        .toList();
```

Both approaches can produce the same result, but they express the computation differently. Modern programming languages often support multiple programming paradigms, so imperative and declarative styles can be used together.

A class is a blueprint that defines the data and behavior of an object. An object is a concrete instance of that class.

For example, a Node class can represent an individual element in a linked list. Each Node object can store a value and a reference to the next node.

```java
class Node {
    int value;
    Node next;

    Node(int value) {
        this.value = value;
        this.next = null;
    }
}

Node first = new Node(10);
```

Here, Node is the class, while first is an object created from that class. The object contains its own value and next reference.

This class-and-object model is commonly used when implementing DSA structures.

Encapsulation means keeping an object's internal data and implementation details together while controlling how that data can be accessed or modified.

In Java, encapsulation is commonly achieved by making fields private and exposing controlled operations through public methods.

For example, a stack can hide its internal storage and provide only the operations that users need.

```java
class Stack {
    private int[] elements;
    private int top = -1;

    public Stack(int size) {
        elements = new int[size];
    }

    public void push(int value) {
        elements[++top] = value;
    }

    public int peek() {
        return elements[top];
    }
}
```

Code using Stack does not need to directly modify the internal elements array or the top variable. This protects the internal state and keeps the implementation controlled.

Abstraction means exposing the important operations of a component while hiding unnecessary implementation details.

For example, when using a queue, a developer generally needs to know that enqueue() adds an element and dequeue() removes an element. The developer does not necessarily need to know whether the queue internally uses an array, linked list, or another structure.

```java
class Queue {
    public void enqueue(int value) {
        // Add value to the queue
    }

    public int dequeue() {
        // Remove and return the front value
        return 0;
    }
}
```

The public methods provide an abstraction over the internal implementation. This makes the data structure easier to use and allows its implementation to change without necessarily changing the code that uses it.

Inheritance allows one class to acquire accessible fields and methods from another class. The class being inherited from is commonly called the parent or superclass, while the class that inherits from it is called the child or subclass.

For example:

```java
class DataStructure {
    void displayInfo() {
        System.out.println("Data structure");
    }
}

class Stack extends DataStructure {
    void push(int value) {
        System.out.println("Pushing: " + value);
    }
}

Stack stack = new Stack();
stack.displayInfo();
stack.push(10);
```

Stack inherits the displayInfo() method from DataStructure and also provides its own push() method.

Inheritance can be useful for representing relationships between related classes, although composition is often preferred when classes do not have a natural parent-child relationship.

Polymorphism allows code to work with objects through a common type while the actual behavior can vary depending on the object's implementation.

One common form of polymorphism occurs when different classes implement the same interface.

```java
interface DataStructure {
    void add(int value);
}

class Stack implements DataStructure {
    public void add(int value) {
        System.out.println("Adding to stack");
    }
}

class Queue implements DataStructure {
    public void add(int value) {
        System.out.println("Adding to queue");
    }
}

DataStructure structure = new Stack();
structure.add(10);
```

The variable structure is declared using the DataStructure interface, but the actual object is a Stack. When add() is called, the Stack implementation is executed.

Polymorphism allows algorithms and application code to depend on abstractions instead of tightly coupling themselves to a specific implementation.

An interface defines a contract that specifies operations a class must provide. The interface describes what an implementation can do without requiring the interface itself to define all implementation details.

For example, a data structure interface could define basic operations that different data structures implement in their own way.

```java
interface Collection {
    void add(int value);
    void remove(int value);
    boolean contains(int value);
}

class ArrayCollection implements Collection {
    public void add(int value) {
        // Array-based implementation
    }

    public void remove(int value) {
        // Remove from array
    }

    public boolean contains(int value) {
        // Search array
        return false;
    }
}
```

The interface defines the expected behavior, while ArrayCollection provides the implementation. This separation makes it easier to replace or extend implementations.

Composition means building a class by combining objects or components rather than relying primarily on inheritance. A class contains or uses other objects to perform its work.

Data structures frequently use composition. For example, a linked list can contain Node objects, while a graph can contain collections representing vertices and their connections.

```java
class Node {
    int value;
    Node next;

    Node(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }
}

class LinkedList {
    private Node head;

    public void add(int value) {
        Node newNode = new Node(value);
        newNode.next = head;
        head = newNode;
    }
}
```

The LinkedList class is composed using Node objects. The list does not inherit from Node; instead, it uses Node objects as part of its internal representation.

A constructor is a special method used to initialize an object when it is created. Constructors can receive parameters and use those parameters to establish the initial state of the object.

For example, a tree node can initialize its value and child references through its constructor.

```java
class TreeNode {
    int value;
    TreeNode left;
    TreeNode right;

    TreeNode(int value) {
        this.value = value;
        this.left = null;
        this.right = null;
    }
}

TreeNode root = new TreeNode(50);
```

When root is created, the constructor initializes its value to 50 and its child references to null. Constructors are commonly used when creating nodes for trees, linked lists, and graph structures.

Access modifiers control where classes, fields, methods, and constructors can be accessed. Java provides several access levels, including public, private, protected, and package-private.

For example, a data structure can keep its internal implementation private while exposing only the operations required by its users.

```java
class LinkedList {
    private Node head;

    public void add(int value) {
        // Add a node
    }

    public int size() {
        // Return number of elements
        return 0;
    }
}
```

Here, head is private, so external code cannot directly modify it. The public methods provide controlled access to the data structure.

Using appropriate access modifiers supports encapsulation and helps prevent accidental changes to internal state.

Object-oriented programming provides a natural way to organize implementations of many data structures. A linked list can contain Node objects, a binary tree can contain TreeNode objects, and a graph can be represented using vertex and edge objects or adjacency collections.

Methods can represent operations such as insertion, deletion, searching, and traversal.

For example, a binary tree can be represented using a TreeNode class:

```java
class TreeNode {
    int value;
    TreeNode left;
    TreeNode right;

    TreeNode(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }
}

class BinaryTree {
    TreeNode root;

    void insert(int value) {
        // Insert value into the tree
    }

    void search(int value) {
        // Search for value
    }
}
```

The TreeNode class represents individual nodes, while BinaryTree manages the overall structure and its operations. This separation keeps the implementation organized.

Once the algorithm has been expressed in pseudocode, it can be translated into a specific programming language. The developer maps general operations such as SET, IF, FOR, RETURN, and FUNCTION to the syntax of the chosen language.

For example, this pseudocode:

```text
FUNCTION findMaximum(array)
    SET max = array[0]

    FOR each number in array
        IF number > max
            SET max = number
        END IF
    END FOR

    RETURN max
END FUNCTION
```

Can be implemented in Java as:

```java
static int findMaximum(int[] array) {
    int max = array[0];

    for (int number : array) {
        if (number > max) {
            max = number;
        }
    }

    return max;
}
```

The syntax changes, but the algorithm remains the same. This is one of the main benefits of writing pseudocode before implementation.

A node is the fundamental building block of a linked list. In a singly linked list, a node generally contains two pieces of information: the stored value and a reference to the next node.

In Java, a simple node can be represented using a class:

```java
class Node {
    int data;
    Node next;

    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
```

A node can then be created as:

```java
Node first = new Node(10);
```

The data field stores the actual value, while next stores a reference to another Node. When next is null, the node does not point to another node.

A linked list can be created by connecting multiple nodes through their next references. The head variable stores a reference to the first node.

For example:

```java
Node head = new Node(10);
Node second = new Node(20);
Node third = new Node(30);

head.next = second;
second.next = third;
```

The resulting structure is:

```text
head
 ↓
10 → 20 → 30 → null
```

The nodes do not need to be physically adjacent in memory. The references stored inside the nodes establish the logical order of the list.

Traversal means visiting the nodes of a linked list one by one. Starting from the head, the algorithm follows each next reference until it reaches null.

A typical traversal can be implemented as:

```java
static void printList(Node head) {
    Node current = head;

    while (current != null) {
        System.out.println(current.data);
        current = current.next;
    }
}
```

For the list:

```text
10 → 20 → 30 → null
```

The traversal visits 10, then 20, then 30, and finally stops when current becomes null.

If the list contains n nodes, traversal takes O(n) time because each node is visited once. The iterative implementation uses O(1) additional space.

To insert a node at the end of a singly linked list, the algorithm normally traverses the list until it reaches the final node. The final node's next reference is then updated to point to the new node.

For example:

```text
Before:
10 → 20 → 30 → null

Insert 40

After:
10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → null
```

A simple implementation is:

```java
static Node insertAtEnd(Node head, int value) {
    Node newNode = new Node(value);

    if (head == null) {
        return newNode;
    }

    Node current = head;

    while (current.next != null) {
        current = current.next;
    }

    current.next = newNode;
    return head;
}
```

Without a tail reference, insertion at the end requires traversal and therefore takes O(n) time. If the list maintains a tail reference, the operation can be reduced to O(1).

Deleting the last node from a singly linked list generally requires finding the node immediately before the last node. That node's next reference can then be changed to null.

For example:

```text
Before:
10 → 20 → 30 → null

After deleting 30:
10 → 20 → null
```

A possible implementation is:

```java
static Node deleteLast(Node head) {
    if (head == null || head.next == null) {
        return null;
    }

    Node current = head;

    while (current.next.next != null) {
        current = current.next;
    }

    current.next = null;
    return head;
}
```

Because the list must be traversed to find the previous node, this operation takes O(n) time for a singly linked list when only the head reference is available.

Searching a linked list means visiting nodes until the required value is found or the end of the list is reached.

For example:

```java
static boolean contains(Node head, int target) {
    Node current = head;

    while (current != null) {
        if (current.data == target) {
            return true;
        }

        current = current.next;
    }

    return false;
}
```

Because a singly linked list does not generally provide direct random access by index, searching may require visiting every node.

Therefore, the worst-case time complexity of searching is O(n). The best case is O(1) when the target is found in the first node.

The length of a linked list is the number of nodes it contains. It can be calculated by traversing the list and maintaining a counter.

```java
static int length(Node head) {
    int count = 0;
    Node current = head;

    while (current != null) {
        count++;
        current = current.next;
    }

    return count;
}
```

For a list containing:

```text
10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → null
```

The method returns 4.

The traversal visits every node, so calculating the length this way takes O(n) time and O(1) additional space.

Reversing a linked list changes the direction of every next reference. For example:

```text
Before:
10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → null

After:
40 → 30 → 20 → 10 → null
```

An iterative solution commonly uses three references: previous, current, and next.

```java
static Node reverse(Node head) {
    Node previous = null;
    Node current = head;

    while (current != null) {
        Node next = current.next;
        current.next = previous;
        previous = current;
        current = next;
    }

    return previous;
}
```

The next reference is saved before changing current.next. This prevents losing access to the remaining nodes.

Every node is processed once, so the time complexity is O(n), while the iterative approach uses O(1) additional space.

A doubly linked list stores references to both the next and previous nodes. This allows traversal in both directions.

A typical structure looks like:

```text
null ← 10 ⇄ 20 ⇄ 30 → null
```

A Java node can be represented as:

```java
class DoublyNode {
    int data;
    DoublyNode previous;
    DoublyNode next;

    DoublyNode(int data) {
        this.data = data;
    }
}
```

The previous reference allows an algorithm to move backward without starting again from the head.

The trade-off is additional memory and more reference updates during insertion and deletion.

In a circular linked list, the last node points back to the first node instead of pointing to null.

For example:

```text
10 → 20 → 30
↑         ↓
└─────────┘
```

The last node's next reference points back to the head. Therefore, traversal must use a different stopping condition because there is no null reference marking the end.

A circular list can be traversed using:

```java
static void printCircular(Node head) {
    if (head == null) {
        return;
    }

    Node current = head;

    do {
        System.out.println(current.data);
        current = current.next;
    } while (current != head);
}
```

Circular linked lists can be useful for applications where processing needs to repeatedly cycle through elements.

Arrays and linked lists are both linear data structures, but they have different performance characteristics and memory models.

Arrays provide efficient random access because an element can generally be accessed directly using its index:

```java
int value = numbers[index];
```

A linked list must usually be traversed from the head to reach a particular position:

```java
Node current = head;

for (int i = 0; i < index && current != null; i++) {
    current = current.next;
}
```

Arrays are generally more suitable when frequent random access is required. Linked lists can be useful when frequent insertion or deletion occurs at positions where the required node reference is already available.

The appropriate structure depends on the operations the application performs most frequently.

Linked-list implementations are particularly sensitive to reference-management errors. A small mistake can disconnect part of the list or create an unintended cycle.

Common mistakes include forgetting to update the head, changing a reference before saving the remainder of the list, dereferencing null, and using the wrong stopping condition.

For example, when inserting a node, the safe order is generally:

```java
Node newNode = new Node(value);

newNode.next = current.next;
current.next = newNode;
```

The original current.next is preserved by assigning it to newNode.next before current.next is changed.

When deleting or reversing nodes, always consider whether the operation could make part of the list unreachable or create an incorrect reference structure.

Each BST node normally contains three pieces of information: the value stored in the node, a reference to the left child, and a reference to the right child.

A simple Java representation looks like this:

```java
class Node {
    int value;
    Node left;
    Node right;

    Node(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }
}
```

The left and right references may be null when the node does not have the corresponding child.

A recursive search can be implemented directly from the BST property.

```java
boolean search(Node root, int target) {
    if (root == null) {
        return false;
    }

    if (root.value == target) {
        return true;
    }

    if (target < root.value) {
        return search(root.left, target);
    }

    return search(root.right, target);
}
```

The method stops when it finds the target or reaches a null reference. The recursive calls always move toward only one subtree.

Searching does not require recursion. An iterative approach can use a loop and a current-node reference.

```java
boolean search(Node root, int target) {
    Node current = root;

    while (current != null) {
        if (current.value == target) {
            return true;
        }

        if (target < current.value) {
            current = current.left;
        } else {
            current = current.right;
        }
    }

    return false;
}
```

The iterative version avoids recursive call-stack usage and is useful when the tree can become very deep.

A recursive insertion method can return the root of the updated subtree.

```java
Node insert(Node root, int value) {
    if (root == null) {
        return new Node(value);
    }

    if (value < root.value) {
        root.left = insert(root.left, value);
    } else if (value > root.value) {
        root.right = insert(root.right, value);
    }

    return root;
}
```

The method ignores duplicate values in this implementation. Another BST design may choose to store duplicates using a separate count or a consistent left/right rule.

The minimum value in a BST is found by repeatedly following the left child. The leftmost node contains the smallest value because every left child is smaller than its parent.

For example:

```text
        50
       /  \
     30    70
    /
   20
  /
 10
```

The minimum value is 10.

An iterative implementation is straightforward:

```java
int findMin(Node root) {
    if (root == null) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Tree is empty");
    }

    Node current = root;

    while (current.left != null) {
        current = current.left;
    }

    return current.value;
}
```

The maximum value is found by repeatedly following the right child. The rightmost node contains the largest value in the BST.

```java
int findMax(Node root) {
    if (root == null) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Tree is empty");
    }

    Node current = root;

    while (current.right != null) {
        current = current.right;
    }

    return current.value;
}
```

The minimum operation follows the leftmost path, while the maximum operation follows the rightmost path.

The following implementation handles all three deletion cases: leaf nodes, nodes with one child, and nodes with two children.

```java
Node delete(Node root, int value) {
    if (root == null) {
        return null;
    }

    if (value < root.value) {
        root.left = delete(root.left, value);
    } else if (value > root.value) {
        root.right = delete(root.right, value);
    } else {
        if (root.left == null) {
            return root.right;
        }

        if (root.right == null) {
            return root.left;
        }

        Node successor = root.right;
        while (successor.left != null) {
            successor = successor.left;
        }

        root.value = successor.value;
        root.right = delete(root.right, successor.value);
    }

    return root;
}
```

The important part is that the replacement value must preserve the BST ordering property.

A min-heap insertion can be implemented using an ArrayList. The new value is appended and then moved upward while it is smaller than its parent.

```java
void insert(int value) {
    heap.add(value);

    int index = heap.size() - 1;

    while (index > 0) {
        int parent = (index - 1) / 2;

        if (heap.get(parent) <= heap.get(index)) {
            break;
        }

        int temp = heap.get(parent);
        heap.set(parent, heap.get(index));
        heap.set(index, temp);

        index = parent;
    }
}
```

The loop stops when the new element reaches a valid position or becomes the root.

The following method removes the minimum element from a min-heap and restores the heap property.

```java
int removeMin() {
    if (heap.isEmpty()) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Heap is empty");
    }

    int result = heap.get(0);
    int last = heap.remove(heap.size() - 1);

    if (!heap.isEmpty()) {
        heap.set(0, last);
        heapifyDown(0);
    }

    return result;
}

void heapifyDown(int index) {
    while (true) {
        int left = 2 * index + 1;
        int right = 2 * index + 2;
        int smallest = index;

        if (left < heap.size() && heap.get(left) < heap.get(smallest)) {
            smallest = left;
        }

        if (right < heap.size() && heap.get(right) < heap.get(smallest)) {
            smallest = right;
        }

        if (smallest == index) {
            break;
        }

        int temp = heap.get(index);
        heap.set(index, heap.get(smallest));
        heap.set(smallest, temp);

        index = smallest;
    }
}
```

The peek operation returns the root element without removing it.

For a min-heap, peek returns the minimum element. For a max-heap, it returns the maximum element.

Because the root is stored at array index 0, peek is an O(1) operation.

```java
int peek() {
    if (heap.isEmpty()) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Heap is empty");
    }

    return heap.get(0);
}
```

A min-heap can be built from an array using bottom-up heapification.

```java
void buildHeap(int[] values) {
    for (int i = values.length / 2 - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        heapifyDown(values, i);
    }
}

void heapifyDown(int[] values, int index) {
    int size = values.length;

    while (true) {
        int left = 2 * index + 1;
        int right = 2 * index + 2;
        int smallest = index;

        if (left < size && values[left] < values[smallest]) {
            smallest = left;
        }

        if (right < size && values[right] < values[smallest]) {
            smallest = right;
        }

        if (smallest == index) {
            return;
        }

        int temp = values[index];
        values[index] = values[smallest];
        values[smallest] = temp;

        index = smallest;
    }
}
```

The bottom-up approach builds a heap in O(n) time.

Java provides PriorityQueue for common heap-based operations. By default, it behaves as a min-priority queue.

```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();

minHeap.add(40);
minHeap.add(10);
minHeap.add(30);
minHeap.add(20);

System.out.println(minHeap.peek());
System.out.println(minHeap.poll());
```

The first peek returns 10 because it is the smallest element. The poll operation removes and returns the smallest element.

Java's PriorityQueue can also behave as a max-heap by providing a reverse-order comparator.

```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Collections;

PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

maxHeap.add(40);
maxHeap.add(10);
maxHeap.add(30);
maxHeap.add(20);

System.out.println(maxHeap.peek());
System.out.println(maxHeap.poll());
```

Here the largest element, 40, is returned first.

Java provides the Set interface in the java.util package. HashSet is one of the most commonly used implementations.

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

Set<Integer> numbers = new HashSet<>();

numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(20);
numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(30);

System.out.println(numbers);
```

Although 10 is added twice, the set stores it only once.

The exact iteration order of a HashSet should not be relied upon.

The add operation inserts an element into a set. If the element already exists, the set remains unchanged.

```java
Set<String> names = new HashSet<>();

names.add("Amit");
names.add("Neha");
names.add("Amit");

System.out.println(names.size());
```

The output is 2 because the second attempt to add "Amit" does not create another copy.

The boolean result of add can also tell us whether the set actually changed.

The contains operation checks whether an element is present in the set.

```java
Set<Integer> numbers = new HashSet<>();

numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(20);
numbers.add(30);

if (numbers.contains(20)) {
    System.out.println("20 exists");
}
```

Membership checking is one of the most important use cases for sets. With a properly implemented hash-based set, contains is typically O(1) on average.

The remove operation deletes an element from the set if it exists.

```java
Set<Integer> numbers = new HashSet<>();

numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(20);
numbers.add(30);

numbers.remove(20);

System.out.println(numbers.contains(20));
```

The result is false because 20 has been removed.

Removing an element that does not exist normally leaves the set unchanged.

The size operation returns the number of unique elements in a set. The isEmpty operation checks whether the set contains no elements.

```java
Set<String> languages = new HashSet<>();

languages.add("Java");
languages.add("Python");

System.out.println(languages.size());
System.out.println(languages.isEmpty());
```

The size is 2 and isEmpty returns false.

A set can be traversed using an enhanced for loop.

```java
Set<Integer> numbers = new HashSet<>();

numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(20);
numbers.add(30);

for (int number : numbers) {
    System.out.println(number);
}
```

When using HashSet, do not assume that elements will be returned in insertion order or sorted order. If ordering matters, choose an appropriate implementation such as LinkedHashSet or TreeSet.

One of the most common practical uses of a set is removing duplicate values from a collection.

Suppose an array contains:

```text
[10, 20, 10, 30, 20, 40]
```

Adding every value to a set produces:

```text
[10, 20, 30, 40]
```

In Java:

```java
int[] values = {10, 20, 10, 30, 20, 40};
Set<Integer> unique = new HashSet<>();

for (int value : values) {
    unique.add(value);
}

System.out.println(unique);
```

The set automatically prevents duplicate entries.

HashSet is a hash-table-based implementation of the Set interface. It is designed for fast insertion, removal, and membership checking.

```java
Set<Integer> numbers = new HashSet<>();

numbers.add(50);
numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(30);

System.out.println(numbers.contains(30));
```

HashSet does not guarantee a specific iteration order.

For typical use cases, add, remove, and contains have O(1) average time complexity, although the exact performance depends on hashing and implementation details.

LinkedHashSet is similar to HashSet but additionally maintains insertion order during iteration.

```java
Set<String> languages = new LinkedHashSet<>();

languages.add("Java");
languages.add("Python");
languages.add("Go");

for (String language : languages) {
    System.out.println(language);
}
```

The elements are iterated in the order they were inserted.

LinkedHashSet is useful when we need uniqueness while also preserving insertion order.

TreeSet is a sorted implementation of Set. It stores unique elements according to their natural ordering or a supplied Comparator.

```java
Set<Integer> numbers = new TreeSet<>();

numbers.add(40);
numbers.add(10);
numbers.add(30);
numbers.add(20);

System.out.println(numbers);
```

The elements are maintained in sorted order.

TreeSet is typically implemented using a balanced tree structure, so operations such as add, remove, and contains generally take O(log n) time.

The union of two sets contains every unique element that appears in either set.

For example:

```text
A = {1, 2, 3}
B = {3, 4, 5}

A ∪ B = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
```

In Java, union can be performed using addAll.

```java
Set<Integer> a = new HashSet<>(Set.of(1, 2, 3));
Set<Integer> b = Set.of(3, 4, 5);

a.addAll(b);

System.out.println(a);
```

The result contains each value only once.

The intersection of two sets contains only the elements that exist in both sets.

For example:

```text
A = {1, 2, 3}
B = {2, 3, 4}

A ∩ B = {2, 3}
```

In Java, retainAll can be used to keep only common elements.

```java
Set<Integer> a = new HashSet<>(Set.of(1, 2, 3));
Set<Integer> b = Set.of(2, 3, 4);

a.retainAll(b);

System.out.println(a);
```

The difference between two sets contains elements that are present in the first set but not in the second.

For example:

```text
A = {1, 2, 3}
B = {2, 3, 4}

A - B = {1}
```

In Java, removeAll can perform this operation.

```java
Set<Integer> a = new HashSet<>(Set.of(1, 2, 3));
Set<Integer> b = Set.of(2, 3, 4);

a.removeAll(b);

System.out.println(a);
```

Two sets are considered equal when they contain exactly the same elements, regardless of their iteration order.

```java
Set<Integer> first = new HashSet<>(Set.of(10, 20, 30));
Set<Integer> second = new HashSet<>(Set.of(30, 10, 20));

System.out.println(first.equals(second));
```

The result is true because both sets contain the same unique elements.

A set A is a subset of B when every element in A is also present in B.

For example:

```text
A = {2, 4}
B = {2, 4, 6, 8}

A is a subset of B.
```

Java provides containsAll for this check:

```java
Set<Integer> a = Set.of(2, 4);
Set<Integer> b = Set.of(2, 4, 6, 8);

System.out.println(b.containsAll(a));
```

The result is true because every element of A exists in B.

Sets can store custom objects, but equality must be defined correctly when logical duplicates are involved.

```java
class User {
    private final int id;

    User(int id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        if (this == obj) return true;
        if (!(obj instanceof User other)) return false;
        return id == other.id;
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Integer.hashCode(id);
    }
}
```

With this implementation, two User objects having the same id are considered equal by a HashSet.

Sets appear in many practical programming problems.

Common examples include detecting duplicate values, checking whether an item has already been processed, tracking visited nodes during graph traversal, finding common elements between collections, removing duplicate records, and validating whether a collection contains only unique values.

For example, a graph traversal can use a set to remember visited nodes:

```java
Set<Integer> visited = new HashSet<>();

if (!visited.contains(node)) {
    visited.add(node);
    // Process node
}
```

This prevents the same node from being processed repeatedly.

Peek returns the front element without removing it.

```java
Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();

queue.add(10);
queue.add(20);
queue.add(30);

System.out.println(queue.peek());
System.out.println(queue.size());
```

The peek operation returns 10, while the size of the queue remains 3.

In Java, peek returns null when the queue is empty, while element throws an exception. The choice depends on how the application wants to handle an empty queue.

A simple linked-list-based queue can be implemented with separate front and rear references.

```java
class Queue {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;

        Node(int value) {
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    private Node front;
    private Node rear;

    void enqueue(int value) {
        Node node = new Node(value);

        if (rear == null) {
            front = rear = node;
            return;
        }

        rear.next = node;
        rear = node;
    }

    int dequeue() {
        if (front == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Queue is empty");
        }

        int value = front.value;
        front = front.next;

        if (front == null) {
            rear = null;
        }

        return value;
    }
}
```

When the last element is removed, both front and rear must become null.

A circular queue can be implemented with an array, front index, rear index, and size.

```java
class CircularQueue {
    private final int[] data;
    private int front = 0;
    private int rear = 0;
    private int size = 0;

    CircularQueue(int capacity) {
        data = new int[capacity];
    }

    void enqueue(int value) {
        if (size == data.length) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Queue is full");
        }

        data[rear] = value;
        rear = (rear + 1) % data.length;
        size++;
    }

    int dequeue() {
        if (size == 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Queue is empty");
        }

        int value = data[front];
        front = (front + 1) % data.length;
        size--;

        return value;
    }
}
```

The modulo operation allows both indexes to wrap around when they reach the end of the array.

Java provides the Deque interface and implementations such as ArrayDeque.

```java
Deque<Integer> deque = new ArrayDeque<>();

deque.addFirst(20);
deque.addLast(30);
deque.addFirst(10);

deque.removeFirst();
deque.removeLast();
```

ArrayDeque is commonly used when efficient operations are needed at both ends of a collection.

Java's PriorityQueue can be used when elements need to be processed according to priority.

```java
PriorityQueue<Integer> queue = new PriorityQueue<>();

queue.add(30);
queue.add(10);
queue.add(20);

System.out.println(queue.peek());
System.out.println(queue.poll());
```

peek returns 10, and poll removes and returns 10.

For a max-priority queue, a reverse-order comparator can be supplied.

A queue can be used to implement BFS on a graph or tree.

```java
Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
Set<Integer> visited = new HashSet<>();

queue.offer(start);
visited.add(start);

while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int node = queue.poll();

    for (int neighbor : graph.get(node)) {
        if (!visited.contains(neighbor)) {
            visited.add(neighbor);
            queue.offer(neighbor);
        }
    }
}
```

The queue controls the order in which discovered nodes are processed, while the visited set prevents repeated processing.

Height is important because AVL balancing is based on subtree heights.

A common implementation stores the height directly inside every node so that it can be updated efficiently after an insertion or deletion.

One possible node structure is:

```java
class Node {
    int value;
    int height;
    Node left;
    Node right;

    Node(int value) {
        this.value = value;
        this.height = 1;
    }
}
```

Here a newly created leaf node has height 1. Some implementations define leaf height as 0 instead. Either convention is valid as long as the implementation uses it consistently.

The height of a node can be calculated from the heights of its children.

Using the convention that a null child has height 0:

```java
int height(Node node) {
    return node == null ? 0 : node.height;
}

void updateHeight(Node node) {
    node.height = 1 + Math.max(height(node.left), height(node.right));
}
```

After a tree modification, the height of each affected ancestor must be updated before its balance factor is checked.

The balance factor can be calculated with a small helper method.

```java
int getBalance(Node node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return 0;
    }

    return height(node.left) - height(node.right);
}
```

This value is checked after modifying the tree. If it becomes greater than 1 or less than -1, the corresponding subtree must be rebalanced.

A right rotation can be implemented by changing a small number of references.

```java
Node rotateRight(Node y) {
    Node x = y.left;
    Node middle = x.right;

    x.right = y;
    y.left = middle;

    updateHeight(y);
    updateHeight(x);

    return x;
}
```

The returned node becomes the new root of this subtree. Heights must be updated after the references are changed.

The left rotation is the mirror image of the right rotation.

```java
Node rotateLeft(Node x) {
    Node y = x.right;
    Node middle = y.left;

    y.left = x;
    x.right = middle;

    updateHeight(x);
    updateHeight(y);

    return y;
}
```

The new subtree root is returned so the parent can update its child reference correctly.

A complete recursive insertion implementation can combine BST insertion, height updates, balance checking, and rotations.

```java
Node insert(Node root, int value) {
    if (root == null) {
        return new Node(value);
    }

    if (value < root.value) {
        root.left = insert(root.left, value);
    } else if (value > root.value) {
        root.right = insert(root.right, value);
    } else {
        return root;
    }

    updateHeight(root);

    int balance = getBalance(root);

    if (balance > 1 && value < root.left.value) {
        return rotateRight(root);
    }

    if (balance < -1 && value > root.right.value) {
        return rotateLeft(root);
    }

    if (balance > 1 && value > root.left.value) {
        root.left = rotateLeft(root.left);
        return rotateRight(root);
    }

    if (balance < -1 && value < root.right.value) {
        root.right = rotateRight(root.right);
        return rotateLeft(root);
    }

    return root;
}
```

The method returns the new root of the subtree because a rotation can change the subtree's root.

A simple optimized Bubble Sort implementation can stop early when no swaps occur during a pass.

```java
void bubbleSort(int[] arr) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
        boolean swapped = false;

        for (int j = 0; j < arr.length - 1 - i; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
                swapped = true;
            }
        }

        if (!swapped) {
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

The optimization allows Bubble Sort to finish in O(n) time when the input is already sorted.

Selection Sort can be implemented by finding the minimum value in each remaining portion of the array.

```java
void selectionSort(int[] arr) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {
        int minIndex = i;

        for (int j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < arr[minIndex]) {
                minIndex = j;
            }
        }

        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[minIndex];
        arr[minIndex] = temp;
    }
}
```

Selection Sort performs relatively few swaps compared with some other simple quadratic algorithms, but it still requires O(n²) comparisons.

Insertion Sort shifts larger elements to the right until the correct position for the current value is found.

```java
void insertionSort(int[] arr) {
    for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
        int current = arr[i];
        int j = i - 1;

        while (j >= 0 && arr[j] > current) {
            arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
            j--;
        }

        arr[j + 1] = current;
    }
}
```

The algorithm maintains the invariant that the portion before index i is sorted.

The merge step combines two already sorted portions into one sorted portion.

```java
void mergeSort(int[] arr, int left, int right) {
    if (left >= right) {
        return;
    }

    int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

    mergeSort(arr, left, mid);
    mergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right);

    merge(arr, left, mid, right);
}
```

A complete implementation also needs a merge method that combines the two sorted ranges. Merge Sort generally requires O(n) auxiliary space for arrays.

A common implementation chooses the last element as the pivot and partitions the array around it.

```java
void quickSort(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
    if (low >= high) {
        return;
    }

    int pivotIndex = partition(arr, low, high);

    quickSort(arr, low, pivotIndex - 1);
    quickSort(arr, pivotIndex + 1, high);
}

int partition(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
    int pivot = arr[high];
    int i = low;

    for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
        if (arr[j] < pivot) {
            int temp = arr[i];
            arr[i] = arr[j];
            arr[j] = temp;
            i++;
        }
    }

    int temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[high];
    arr[high] = temp;

    return i;
}
```

The choice of pivot can significantly affect Quick Sort performance.

For non-negative integers within a manageable range, Counting Sort can be implemented using a frequency array.

```java
void countingSort(int[] arr) {
    if (arr.length == 0) {
        return;
    }

    int max = arr[0];

    for (int value : arr) {
        max = Math.max(max, value);
    }

    int[] count = new int[max + 1];

    for (int value : arr) {
        count[value]++;
    }

    int index = 0;

    for (int value = 0; value < count.length; value++) {
        while (count[value] > 0) {
            arr[index++] = value;
            count[value]--;
        }
    }
}
```

This basic version assumes that all values are non-negative.

In real applications, it is usually better to use well-tested library sorting implementations instead of manually implementing a sorting algorithm unless the algorithm itself is the subject of the problem.

For primitive arrays, Java provides Arrays.sort.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

int[] numbers = {40, 10, 30, 20, 50};

Arrays.sort(numbers);

System.out.println(Arrays.toString(numbers));
```

For objects or collections, Java also provides sorting utilities such as List.sort and Collections.sort. The exact implementation depends on the data type and API being used.

Objects can be sorted according to a specific field by using a Comparator.

```java
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("Ravi");
names.add("Amit");
names.add("Neha");

names.sort(Comparator.naturalOrder());

System.out.println(names);
```

For custom objects, a comparator can define exactly which property should determine the ordering.

For example, employees could be sorted by salary, name, joining date, or any combination of fields.

A factor of a number is an integer that divides the number without leaving a remainder.

For example, the factors of 12 are:

```text
1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12
```

A straightforward approach checks every value from 1 through n. However, factors occur in pairs, so we only need to check up to the square root of n.

If i divides n, then both i and n / i are factors.

```java
void printFactors(int n) {
    for (int i = 1; i * i <= n; i++) {
        if (n % i == 0) {
            System.out.println(i);

            if (i != n / i) {
                System.out.println(n / i);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This reduces the number of checks from O(n) to O(sqrt(n)).

To determine whether n is prime, it is enough to check possible divisors up to sqrt(n).

If n has a divisor greater than sqrt(n), its corresponding paired divisor must be smaller than sqrt(n).

```java
boolean isPrime(int n) {
    if (n < 2) {
        return false;
    }

    if (n == 2) {
        return true;
    }

    if (n % 2 == 0) {
        return false;
    }

    for (int i = 3; i * i <= n; i += 2) {
        if (n % i == 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    return true;
}
```

This approach takes O(sqrt(n)) time in the worst case and avoids checking unnecessary even divisors.

A basic implementation can generate all primes up to a given limit.

```java
boolean[] prime = new boolean[n + 1];
Arrays.fill(prime, true);

if (n >= 0) prime[0] = false;
if (n >= 1) prime[1] = false;

for (int p = 2; p * p <= n; p++) {
    if (prime[p]) {
        for (int multiple = p * p; multiple <= n; multiple += p) {
            prime[multiple] = false;
        }
    }
}
```

Starting from p * p is sufficient because smaller multiples have already been handled by smaller prime numbers.

The Euclidean algorithm repeatedly replaces the larger problem with a smaller remainder problem.

For example:

```text
GCD(48, 18)
48 % 18 = 12
GCD(18, 12)
18 % 12 = 6
GCD(12, 6)
12 % 6 = 0

Answer = 6
```

Java implementation:

```java
int gcd(int a, int b) {
    a = Math.abs(a);
    b = Math.abs(b);

    while (b != 0) {
        int remainder = a % b;
        a = b;
        b = remainder;
    }

    return a;
}
```

The algorithm runs in O(log(min(a, b))) time.

LCM can be implemented using the GCD method.

```java
long lcm(long a, long b) {
    if (a == 0 || b == 0) {
        return 0;
    }

    return Math.abs((a / gcd(a, b)) * b);
}

long gcd(long a, long b) {
    a = Math.abs(a);
    b = Math.abs(b);

    while (b != 0) {
        long remainder = a % b;
        a = b;
        b = remainder;
    }

    return a;
}
```

Using long instead of int provides a larger range, although overflow is still possible for sufficiently large inputs.

Exponentiation by squaring uses the following relationships:

```text
x^n = (x^(n/2))²              when n is even
x^n = x × x^(n-1)             when n is odd
```

An iterative implementation can repeatedly square the base while reducing the exponent by half.

```java
long power(long base, long exponent) {
    long result = 1;

    while (exponent > 0) {
        if ((exponent & 1) == 1) {
            result *= base;
        }

        base *= base;
        exponent >>= 1;
    }

    return result;
}
```

This algorithm performs O(log exponent) iterations.

The number of digits in an integer can be found by repeatedly dividing the absolute value by 10.

For example:

```text
12345 → 1234 → 123 → 12 → 1 → 0
```

There are five digits.

```java
int countDigits(long n) {
    n = Math.abs(n);

    if (n == 0) {
        return 1;
    }

    int count = 0;

    while (n > 0) {
        n /= 10;
        count++;
    }

    return count;
}
```

The time complexity is O(log₁₀ n), which is proportional to the number of digits.

The sum of digits can be calculated by repeatedly extracting the last digit using the modulo operator.

For example:

```text
12345
5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 15
```

The last digit is obtained using n % 10, and then the digit is removed using integer division by 10.

```java
int digitSum(int n) {
    n = Math.abs(n);
    int sum = 0;

    while (n > 0) {
        sum += n % 10;
        n /= 10;
    }

    return sum;
}
```

This pattern is useful in many digit-based programming problems.

A number can be reversed by repeatedly extracting its last digit and adding it to the result.

For example:

```text
1234 → 4321
```

The general update is:

```text
reversed = reversed × 10 + lastDigit
```

Java implementation:

```java
int reverse(int n) {
    int result = 0;

    while (n != 0) {
        int digit = n % 10;
        result = result * 10 + digit;
        n /= 10;
    }

    return result;
}
```

When working with fixed-width integer types, overflow must be considered for sufficiently large inputs.

A number is a palindrome if it reads the same from left to right and right to left.

Examples:

```text
121 → palindrome
1331 → palindrome
123 → not a palindrome
```

One approach is to reverse the number and compare the reversed value with the original.

```java
boolean isPalindrome(int n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        return false;
    }

    int original = n;
    int reversed = 0;

    while (n > 0) {
        reversed = reversed * 10 + n % 10;
        n /= 10;
    }

    return original == reversed;
}
```

For very large integers, a string-based or overflow-safe approach may be preferable.

An iterative Fibonacci algorithm avoids repeated calculations by keeping only the previous two values.

```java
long fibonacci(int n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("n must be non-negative");
    }

    long previous = 0;
    long current = 1;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        long next = previous + current;
        previous = current;
        current = next;
    }

    return previous;
}
```

This runs in O(n) time and O(1) extra space, although fixed-width integer overflow occurs once the Fibonacci values exceed the type's range.

Integer overflow occurs when a calculation produces a value outside the range that the selected integer type can represent.

For example, Java's int type has a maximum value of 2,147,483,647. Adding another positive value can wrap around to a negative value.

```java
int x = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
int y = x + 1;

System.out.println(y);
```

When calculations may exceed the int range, use a wider type such as long or a suitable arbitrary-precision type such as BigInteger.

Numeric algorithms should always consider the possible range of intermediate results, not only the final result.

A basic Linear Search implementation returns the index of the target or -1 when the target does not exist.

```java
int linearSearch(int[] arr, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] == target) {
            return i;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

The algorithm stops as soon as the target is found, so it does not need to inspect the remaining elements.

An iterative implementation avoids recursive call-stack usage.

```java
int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = arr.length - 1;

    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        }

        if (arr[mid] < target) {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            high = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

The expression low + (high - low) / 2 is preferred over (low + high) / 2 because it reduces the risk of integer overflow.

When duplicate values exist, standard Binary Search may return any matching occurrence. Sometimes the problem requires the first occurrence specifically.

For example:

```text
[10, 20, 20, 20, 30]
      ↑
first occurrence
```

When the target is found, instead of immediately returning, continue searching toward the left while remembering the current answer.

```java
int firstOccurrence(int[] arr, int target) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = arr.length - 1;
    int answer = -1;

    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            answer = mid;
            high = mid - 1;
        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            high = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    return answer;
}
```

The complexity remains O(log n).

The same idea can be used to find the last occurrence of a duplicate value.

When the target is found, store the index and continue searching toward the right.

```java
int lastOccurrence(int[] arr, int target) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = arr.length - 1;
    int answer = -1;

    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            answer = mid;
            low = mid + 1;
        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            high = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    return answer;
}
```

This is another example of how Binary Search can be adapted to answer more than simple existence questions.

A basic Jump Search implementation can use a block size based on the square root of the array length.

```java
int jumpSearch(int[] arr, int target) {
    int n = arr.length;
    int block = (int) Math.sqrt(n);
    int start = 0;
    int end = block;

    while (start < n && arr[Math.min(end, n) - 1] < target) {
        start = end;
        end += block;

        if (start >= n) {
            return -1;
        }
    }

    for (int i = start; i < Math.min(end, n); i++) {
        if (arr[i] == target) {
            return i;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

Jump Search requires sorted data and is generally less common than Binary Search in practice.

Searching is not limited to numeric arrays. Strings can also be searched for characters, words, prefixes, or substrings.

For simple substring searching, Java provides methods such as contains and indexOf.

```java
String text = "Java makes searching interesting";

int index = text.indexOf("searching");

System.out.println(index);
```

For large-scale string matching problems, specialized algorithms such as KMP, Rabin-Karp, and Z-algorithm can reduce unnecessary comparisons.

A hash-based data structure such as HashSet or HashMap can provide very fast average-case membership checks.

For example:

```java
Set<Integer> values = new HashSet<>();
values.add(10);
values.add(20);
values.add(30);

if (values.contains(20)) {
    System.out.println("Found");
}
```

Hash-based lookup is typically O(1) on average, although worst-case behavior depends on the implementation and hashing characteristics.

Unlike Binary Search, hash-based searching does not require the values to be sorted.

Java provides the `String` class for working with text. Strings are objects and are commonly created using string literals.

```java
String name = "Suresh";
String language = "Java";
```

Java strings are immutable, which means that once a `String` object has been created, its contents cannot be changed. Operations that appear to modify a string actually create another string when necessary.

For example:

```java
String value = "Hello";
value = value + " World";
```

The original string object containing `"Hello"` is not modified. A new string value is produced.

String immutability is an important concept in Java. Once a `String` object exists, its character sequence cannot be changed.

Consider:

```java
String text = "Java";
text.concat(" Programming");

System.out.println(text);
```

The output is still `Java` because `concat()` returns a new string and does not modify the existing object.

To keep the result, the returned value must be assigned:

```java
text = text.concat(" Programming");
```

Immutability makes strings safer to share and provides useful properties for hashing and caching, but repeatedly creating strings can become expensive when performing many modifications.

Many string algorithms begin by visiting each character exactly once. This is called string traversal.

In Java, a traditional loop can access characters using `charAt()`:

```java
String text = "hello";

for (int i = 0; i < text.length(); i++) {
    char ch = text.charAt(i);
    System.out.println(ch);
}
```

The loop runs once for every character, giving a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the string.

Traversal is the foundation for counting characters, validation, searching, filtering, and many other string algorithms.

Common operations include finding the length, accessing characters, extracting substrings, comparing strings, changing case, trimming whitespace, and checking whether a string contains another string.

```java
String text = "Hello World";

System.out.println(text.length());
System.out.println(text.charAt(0));
System.out.println(text.substring(0, 5));
System.out.println(text.toLowerCase());
System.out.println(text.contains("World"));
```

The exact cost of an operation depends on the language and implementation. In algorithm problems, always consider whether an operation creates a new string or scans the existing characters.

String comparison is a frequent source of bugs, especially in Java. The `==` operator compares object references, while `equals()` compares the contents of strings.

Use:

```java
String first = "java";
String second = new String("java");

System.out.println(first.equals(second));
```

This prints `true` because both strings contain the same characters.

For case-insensitive comparison, Java provides `equalsIgnoreCase()`:

```java
System.out.println("Java".equalsIgnoreCase("java"));
```

When solving interview problems, use content-based comparison unless reference identity is specifically required.

Concatenation means joining two or more strings together.

```java
String firstName = "Suresh";
String lastName = "Mali";

String fullName = firstName + " " + lastName;
```

For a small number of concatenations, the `+` operator is convenient. However, repeatedly concatenating strings inside a large loop can create many temporary objects because Java strings are immutable.

For repeated modifications, `StringBuilder` is usually more appropriate.

`StringBuilder` provides a mutable sequence of characters and is useful when a string must be modified repeatedly.

```java
StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();

for (char ch : new char[]{'J', 'a', 'v', 'a'}) {
    builder.append(ch);
}

String result = builder.toString();
System.out.println(result);
```

Common operations include `append()`, `insert()`, `delete()`, `reverse()`, and `setCharAt()`.

Using `StringBuilder` can avoid creating a new immutable `String` object for every modification.

Counting how often each character occurs is one of the most useful string techniques.

For a restricted lowercase English alphabet, an integer array of size 26 is often enough.

```java
String text = "banana";
int[] frequency = new int[26];

for (char ch : text.toCharArray()) {
    frequency[ch - 'a']++;
}

System.out.println(frequency['a' - 'a']);
```

This approach takes O(n) time and O(1) additional space when the alphabet size is fixed at 26.

For arbitrary characters or larger character sets, a `HashMap<Character, Integer>` may be more appropriate.

A hash map is useful when the possible characters are not restricted to a small fixed alphabet.

```java
String text = "programming";
Map<Character, Integer> frequency = new HashMap<>();

for (char ch : text.toCharArray()) {
    frequency.put(ch, frequency.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);
}
```

The average time complexity is O(n), while the space complexity depends on the number of distinct characters.

Hash maps are especially useful when problems involve Unicode characters, arbitrary symbols, or dynamically determined keys.

A palindrome is a string that reads the same from left to right and right to left. Examples include `"level"`, `"radar"`, and `"madam"`.

A simple way to check a palindrome is to compare characters from both ends using two pointers.

```java
public static boolean isPalindrome(String text) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = text.length() - 1;

    while (left < right) {
        if (text.charAt(left) != text.charAt(right)) {
            return false;
        }
        left++;
        right--;
    }

    return true;
}
```

The algorithm runs in O(n) time and uses O(1) additional space.

Two strings are anagrams when they contain the same characters with the same frequencies, regardless of their order.

For example, `"listen"` and `"silent"` are anagrams.

One approach is to count character frequencies in both strings and compare the counts.

```java
public static boolean areAnagrams(String a, String b) {
    if (a.length() != b.length()) {
        return false;
    }

    int[] count = new int[26];

    for (int i = 0; i < a.length(); i++) {
        count[a.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
        count[b.charAt(i) - 'a']--;
    }

    for (int value : count) {
        if (value != 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    return true;
}
```

This approach runs in O(n) time with O(1) additional space for a fixed lowercase alphabet.

A substring is a contiguous sequence of characters inside a string. For `"abcdef"`, examples include `"abc"`, `"cde"`, and `"ef"`.

A substring must preserve the original order and the characters must be adjacent.

In Java:

```java
String text = "abcdef";
String part = text.substring(2, 5);

System.out.println(part);
```

The result is `"cde"`. The ending index passed to `substring()` is exclusive.

Many algorithm problems ask for the longest, shortest, unique, or most frequent substring satisfying a particular condition.

A subsequence is formed by deleting zero or more characters while keeping the remaining characters in their original order. Unlike a substring, the selected characters do not need to be adjacent.

For `"abcde"`, `"ace"` is a subsequence because the characters appear in the same order.

A common way to check whether one string is a subsequence of another is to use two pointers.

```java
public static boolean isSubsequence(String a, String b) {
    int i = 0;

    for (int j = 0; j < b.length() && i < a.length(); j++) {
        if (a.charAt(i) == b.charAt(j)) {
            i++;
        }
    }

    return i == a.length();
}
```

This runs in O(n) time relative to the length of the larger string.

Reversing a string is a common introductory string operation and appears in palindrome, transformation, and parsing problems.

With Java, `StringBuilder` provides a convenient implementation:

```java
String text = "hello";
String reversed = new StringBuilder(text).reverse().toString();

System.out.println(reversed);
```

For algorithm practice, it is also useful to understand how reversal works manually by swapping characters from opposite ends.

When Unicode or multi-code-point characters are involved, reversing visible characters can be more complicated than simply reversing UTF-16 code units.

Removing duplicate characters can be solved using a set to remember which characters have already appeared.

```java
public static String removeDuplicates(String text) {
    Set<Character> seen = new HashSet<>();
    StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();

    for (char ch : text.toCharArray()) {
        if (seen.add(ch)) {
            result.append(ch);
        }
    }

    return result.toString();
}
```

The set provides fast average membership checks. The resulting string preserves the order in which distinct characters first appeared.

String problems often require converting text to lowercase or uppercase before comparison.

```java
String text = "Hello Java";

String lower = text.toLowerCase();
String upper = text.toUpperCase();
```

For interview problems involving only English letters, direct character arithmetic such as checking ranges can sometimes be used. However, Java's case-conversion methods are generally clearer for ordinary text processing.

Be aware that case conversion can have locale-sensitive behavior. For strict ASCII-only algorithm problems, the assumptions of the problem should determine which approach is appropriate.

The naive string matching algorithm checks whether the pattern matches the text starting at each possible position.

```java
public static int findPattern(String text, String pattern) {
    if (pattern.length() == 0) {
        return 0;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i <= text.length() - pattern.length(); i++) {
        int j = 0;

        while (j < pattern.length()
                && text.charAt(i + j) == pattern.charAt(j)) {
            j++;
        }

        if (j == pattern.length()) {
            return i;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

The worst-case complexity is O(n × m), but the algorithm is easy to understand and can be sufficient for small inputs.

A classic sliding-window problem is finding the longest substring containing no repeated characters.

The window expands while characters are unique. When a duplicate appears, the left side moves forward until the duplicate is removed from the active window.

```java
public static int longestUniqueSubstring(String text) {
    Set<Character> window = new HashSet<>();
    int left = 0;
    int best = 0;

    for (int right = 0; right < text.length(); right++) {
        char ch = text.charAt(right);

        while (window.contains(ch)) {
            window.remove(text.charAt(left));
            left++;
        }

        window.add(ch);
        best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
    }

    return best;
}
```

The algorithm processes each character a limited number of times, resulting in O(n) average time.
